    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the solenoids seem okay on the electrical side, but i'm still seeing these weird spikes. could it be something inside the valve body or maybe some debris in the lines messing with the pressure? what else should i look at?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, debris trapped inside the valve body or clogged pressure control passages is a very likely culprit for hydraulic spikes when the solenoids themselves function correctly. This contamination often prevents oil from traveling through the valve body at the intended rate, causing pressure to build up erratically before shifting occurs. You should inspect the pan for sludge, check the line pressure regulator for wear or binding, and verify if the low-pressure line filter is actually clogged as that can cause sudden surges during load changes.",
